One notable strategy to embed prior knowledge about physical systems into machine learning models is physics-informed neural networks (PINNs). These models have been observed to perform well with smaller training datasets and can generalize effectively. However, they tend to be unpredictable and unreliable as they lack a systematic approach to adjusting the complexity of the model. In our study, we apply PINNs for solving two initial value problems from ordinary and partial differential equations. By combining the PINNs with an adaptive refinement technique inspired by the rational approximation of transfer functions, we develop a more reliable method for estimating errors that has been independently validated. This method has been tested with different PINN architectures and applied to a variety of differential equations, demonstrating its potential for improving the accuracy of physical and engineering computations. A comprehensive implementation of our method is available under an open-source license. Furthermore, we showcase the capability of our approach to produce certified bounds for the solution and its derivatives on an example from the field of stochastic differential equations. Ultimately, we outline a range of potential applications for this method, including uncertainty estimation, verification, and detection of numerical errors that cannot be explained. [The code for our method is available at https://github.com/MichaelMirta/cpinns]. $$\mathbf{opyright}$\textTM 2022 by the authors. Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0. (https://apache.org/licenses/) Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0. (https://apache.org/licenses/) \textC (c 2022 by the authors). Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0. (https://apache.org/licenses/) \end{aligned}

This is a summary of our approach to verifying the results of physics-informed neural networks (PINNs), which are designed to incorporate physical systems into these machine learning models. One key challenge with these models is understanding their accuracy as they often require smaller training datasets and can generalize effectively. However, we've discovered that they lack a method to adjust their complexity, which has led us to develop a new approach. We combine the results of PINNs with an adaptive refinement technique inspired by transfer functions to produce more accurate results. We've tested our method with ordinary and partial differential equations and found that it improves the accuracy of the results. Our code for this method is available for free. 

